A stack makes the preorder order easy to follow. Push the right child first and the left child second, so the left side is processed next. Each popped node becomes the next node on the right, and its left pointer is cleared.

For example, with preorder `1, 2, 3, 4, 5`, the links become `1.right = 2`, then `2.right = 3`, and so on. An empty tree is handled before using the stack.

```java
/* class TreeNode {
       int val;
       TreeNode left, right;
       TreeNode(int x) { val = x; }
   }
*/

class Solution {
    public void flatten(TreeNode root) {
        if (root == null) {
            return;
        }

        java.util.Stack<TreeNode> stack = new java.util.Stack<>();
        stack.push(root);

        while (!stack.isEmpty()) {
            TreeNode current = stack.pop();

            if (current.right != null) {
                stack.push(current.right);
            }
            if (current.left != null) {
                stack.push(current.left);
            }

            if (!stack.isEmpty()) {
                current.right = stack.peek();
            }
            current.left = null;
        }
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` because every node is pushed and removed once.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the stack. The tree is changed in place.

The preorder order is root, left subtree, then right subtree. When a node has a left child, its left subtree must be placed before its old right subtree.

The rightmost node of the left subtree is the join point. Save the old right subtree there, move the left subtree to the right, and clear the left pointer. Then continue along the new right chain.

The code also works for a single node and for a tree that only has right children. No new tree nodes are created.

```cpp
/* class TreeNode {
       int val;
       TreeNode *left, *right;
       TreeNode(int x) : val(x), left(NULL), right(NULL) {}
   };
*/

class Solution {
public:
    void flatten(TreeNode* root) {
        TreeNode* current = root;

        while (current != nullptr) {
            if (current->left != nullptr) {
                TreeNode* rightmost = current->left;

                while (rightmost->right != nullptr) {
                    rightmost = rightmost->right;
                }

                rightmost->right = current->right;
                current->right = current->left;
                current->left = nullptr;
            }

            current = current->right;
        }
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`. The pointer walk and rewiring visit the tree in linear time.
Space complexity: `O(1)` besides a few pointers.

The list can also be built from the end. Reverse preorder is right, left, root. A field named `previous` stores the part that has already been linked.

When the current node is reached, its next node is already in `previous`. Set `current.right` to that node, set `current.left` to null, and then move `previous` to the current node.

This changes the input tree directly. The main cost is the recursion depth, which can become large for a very unbalanced tree.

```java
/* class TreeNode {
       int val;
       TreeNode left, right;
       TreeNode(int x) { val = x; }
   }
*/

class Solution {
    private TreeNode previous;

    public void flatten(TreeNode root) {
        previous = null;
        buildFromRight(root);
    }

    private void buildFromRight(TreeNode node) {
        if (node == null) {
            return;
        }

        buildFromRight(node.right);
        buildFromRight(node.left);

        node.right = previous;
        node.left = null;
        previous = node;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`, with one visit per node.
Space complexity: `O(h)` for recursion, where `h` is the tree height. For an empty tree or a leaf, the method returns safely.
